  <h1>India’s Judicial Reforms — Pendency, Access, and Accountability</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>India’s judiciary is hailed as the guardian of the Constitution, yet it faces a mounting crisis of pendency, access, and accountability. In 2024, Indian Express highlighted how delays and structural inefficiencies undermine justice delivery. For UPSC, judicial reforms are a crucial theme in polity, governance, and ethics.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Pending Cases (2024): ~5 crore across all courts (SC, HCs, district).<br/>•	Vacancies: 30% judge shortage in High Courts.<br/>•	Average Disposal Time: Civil cases take 15+ years in lower courts.<br/>•	Technology Use: e-Courts project in progress, but uneven adoption.<br/>•	Global Rank: India ranked low in contract enforcement (Doing Business Index).<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Issues<br/>1.	Case Pendency: Backlog eroding faith in judiciary.<br/>2.	Judicial Vacancies: Delays in appointments worsen caseload.<br/>3.	Access Inequality: Rural poor struggle with legal aid and affordability.<br/>4.	Opaque Collegium System: Lack of transparency in judge selection.<br/>5.	Accountability Deficit: Weak mechanisms to address judicial misconduct.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government & Institutional Measures<br/>•	e-Courts Project: Digital filing, video hearings.<br/>•	National Judicial Appointments Commission (NJAC): Struck down in 2015.<br/>•	Fast-Track Courts: For sexual assault, POCSO cases.<br/>•	Lok Adalats & Gram Nyayalayas: Alternative dispute resolution.<br/>•	All-India Judicial Service (AIJS): Proposed for uniform recruitment.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>•	Technology Adoption: Virtual hearings expanded during COVID.<br/>•	Legal Aid: NALSA improved access for vulnerable groups.<br/>•	Fast-Track Justice: Reduced pendency in some categories.<br/>•	Awareness: Greater media coverage on judicial accountability.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Persistent Gaps<br/>•	Infrastructure: Courts lack basic amenities in many districts.<br/>•	Judge-to-Population Ratio: ~21 per million (vs recommended 50).<br/>•	Limited ADR Use: Arbitration/mediation underutilised.<br/>•	Delays in Collegium: Frequent standoffs with executive.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Rule of Law: Justice delayed = justice denied.<br/>•	Economic Growth: Slow contract enforcement deters investment.<br/>•	Social Justice: Marginalised groups worst hit by delays.<br/>•	Democratic Trust: Credibility of judiciary central to governance.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-II (Polity): Judiciary, reforms, appointments.<br/>•	GS-IV (Ethics): Accountability, justice, transparency.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Justice delayed is justice denied.”<br/>o	“Judicial reforms are the cornerstone of India’s democratic deepening.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Rule of law and institutions.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Fill Vacancies: Speed up appointments, AIJS for entry-level judges.<br/>2.	Infrastructure Boost: More courts, digitalisation, technology integration.<br/>3.	Strengthen ADR: Promote arbitration, mediation, Lok Adalats.<br/>4.	Transparency in Collegium: Publish criteria and reasons for selections.<br/>5.	Judicial Accountability: Independent body for complaints against judges.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>Judicial reforms are vital to sustain democratic legitimacy and rule of law. India must move toward a judiciary that is efficient, transparent, and accessible to all citizens. For UPSC aspirants, this reflects the convergence of polity, governance, and ethics in justice delivery.</p>
